  "text": "**Solution 1 has the correct final answer, though its justification is weak.** The ratio is 1.\n\n- **Why 1 is plausible:** For finite-slope eigensystems, Coleman-type classicality results show that forms of level Γ₁(𝔫℘^r) with slope below the weight bound come from level 𝔫℘. In the Drinfeld setting, Hattori's results on finite-slope Drinfeld cuspforms of higher ℘-power level are, to my recollection, of this stabilization type. So the λ-multiplicity should not grow with r. The ordinary case, where Hida's control theorem gives an r-independent rank, points the same way.\n- **Why Solution 1's argument is shaky:** Its claim that the U_℘-eigenspace is one-dimensional for all r is asserted rather than proved. It also ignores the many old copies coming from level 𝔫℘ into level 𝔫℘^r. The conclusion is right, but the reasoning does not establish it.\n- **Why Solution 2 is wrong:** Its q^{2D(r−1)} comes from counting cosets of a Borel-type subgroup and then guessing that the count transfers to multiplicities. It explicitly says \"I'll go with\" that guess. The index-based heuristic has no mechanism that survives the finite-slope/classicality constraint, so the answer is unsupported.\n\nI could not verify this against the literature in this session, so my confidence is moderate rather than certain.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
